---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { site, michigan } from "../data/site.js";

const path = `/${michigan.slug}/`;
const title = "Tax Advisors in Metro Detroit | Enrolled Agents | Advisant";
const description =
  "Enrolled agent tax preparation and IRS representation for Metro Detroit. Michigan flow-through entity elections, city income tax, and multi-state returns.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "AccountingService",
  "@id": `${site.domain}${path}#office`,
  name: site.gbpName,
  url: site.domain + path,
  telephone: michigan.phone,
  parentOrganization: { "@id": `${site.domain}/#organization` },
  address: {
    "@type": "PostalAddress",
    addressLocality: michigan.city,
    addressRegion: michigan.region,
    addressCountry: "US",
  },
  openingHoursSpecification: [{
    "@type": "OpeningHoursSpecification",
    dayOfWeek: ["Monday","Tuesday","Wednesday","Thursday","Friday"],
    opens: "09:00", closes: "17:00",
  }],
  areaServed: michigan.serviceArea.map((c) => ({
    "@type": "City", name: c, containedInPlace: { "@type": "State", name: "Michigan" },
  })),
};
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path={path} schema={schema}>

  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="formline formline--onDark">Michigan · Enrolled Agents</p>
      <h1>Tax advisors serving Metro Detroit.</h1>
      <p>
        Advisant Financial serves individuals and closely held businesses across Oakland and
        Macomb counties from {michigan.city}. We meet clients by appointment and work
        remotely with documents exchanged through our secure portal.
      </p>
      <div class="hero__actions">
        <a class="btn" href={site.applyUrl}>Schedule a consultation</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href={`tel:${michigan.phone.replace(/\D/g, "")}`}>{michigan.phone}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="formline">Michigan practice</p>
        <h2>What Michigan filers actually get wrong</h2>
        <p>
          Michigan looks straightforward on the surface. A single flat individual income tax
          rate, no county income tax, and a return that fits on two pages. The complications
          sit underneath, and they are the ones that cost people money.
        </p>

        <h2>City income tax</h2>
        <p>
          More than twenty Michigan municipalities levy their own income tax, including
          Detroit, Pontiac, Highland Park, Flint, Lansing, and Grand Rapids. Liability follows
          where the work is performed, not where the taxpayer lives, and the nonresident rate
          differs from the resident rate. A Rochester Hills resident who spends part of the
          week at a Detroit office owes Detroit tax on that portion of wages. Employers do not
          always withhold correctly, particularly since hybrid schedules became normal, and
          the day-count allocation is the part most preparers skip.
        </p>

        <h2>The flow-through entity election</h2>
        <p>
          Michigan allows partnerships and S corporations to elect to pay income tax at the
          entity level, shifting a deduction that would otherwise be limited on the owner's
          Schedule A into a business deduction on the federal return. The election is not
          automatic, it has payment deadlines that do not match the filing deadline, and it is
          not advantageous for every owner group. Whether it helps depends on the mix of
          resident and nonresident owners, other state credits, and the owners' federal
          position. We model it before recommending it rather than electing by default.
        </p>

        <h2>Retirement income</h2>
        <p>
          Michigan's treatment of pension and retirement income has been in transition, with a
          restored subtraction phasing in through the 2026 tax year. The rules differ by birth
          year and by the source of the income, and the phase-in means the correct answer for
          a given retiree changes from one filing season to the next. Returns prepared from
          last year's assumptions frequently understate the subtraction.
        </p>

        <h2>Credits people leave unclaimed</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Homestead property tax credit, where eligibility turns on household resources rather than adjusted gross income</li>
          <li>Home heating credit, which is filed separately and on a different deadline</li>
          <li>Michigan's earned income credit, calculated as a percentage of the federal credit</li>
          <li>Credit for income tax paid to another state, for households working across the Ohio or Indiana line</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Business and employer compliance</h2>
        <p>
          For Michigan employers we handle withholding registration, sales and use tax
          returns, unemployment insurance filings, and the annual report to LARA. For firms
          with remote staff in other states, we work through nexus and registration before it
          becomes a notice rather than after.
        </p>

        <h2>Working with us</h2>
        <p>
          Our Michigan address is not open for walk-in traffic. Consultations are by
          appointment, in person or by video, and documents move through an encrypted client
          portal with e-signature. Most engagements begin with a review of your two most
          recent returns, which usually tells us more in twenty minutes than an hour of
          questions would.
        </p>

        <p class="areas">
          Serving {michigan.serviceArea.join(", ")}, and the surrounding {michigan.metro} area.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="facts">
        <p class="formline">Michigan office</p>
        <dl>
          <dt>Location</dt>
          <dd>{michigan.city}, {michigan.region}</dd>
          <dt>Phone</dt>
          <dd><a href={`tel:${michigan.phone.replace(/\D/g, "")}`}>{michigan.phone}</a></dd>
          <dt>Hours</dt>
          <dd>{michigan.hours}</dd>
          <dt>Meetings</dt>
          <dd>By appointment</dd>
          <dt>Credential</dt>
          <dd>Enrolled Agent (IRS)</dd>
        </dl>
        <a class="btn" href={site.applyUrl}>Schedule a consultation</a>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--sunk">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="formline">Also</p>
      <h2>Our Denver office</h2>
      <p class="measure">
        Advisant also practices in Colorado. If your household or business spans both states,
        the same team handles the allocation and the credit for tax paid to the other state.
      </p>
      <p><a href="/denver-tax-services/">Denver tax services &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>
